=== layout: title-slide

# Lecture 11D

## Chromosome Alterations: Deletions

## Chapter 9

## Dr. Neil Voss

## November 17, 2026

=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Distinguish the four chromosome rearrangements.
- Explain how deletions uncover recessive alleles.
- Infer gene order from overlapping deletions.
- Check a proposed order against every deletion.

=== layout: section

# Alterations of Chromosome Structure

=== layout: one-panel

# *Alterations of Chromosome Structure*

@body

- Breakage of a chromosome can lead to four types of changes in chromosome structure
  - [*Deletion*]{underline=true} removes a chromosomal segment
  - [*Duplication*]{underline=true} repeats a segment
  - [*Inversion*]{underline=true} reverses orientation of a segment within a chromosome
  - [*Translocation*]{underline=true} moves a segment from one chromosome to another

=== layout: one-panel

# *Deletion*

@body

![Slide image 1](assets/lect11d-chromo_alter_1-deletions/deletion_overview.drawn.svg)

A deletion removes a chromosomal segment.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Duplication*

@body

![Slide image 2](assets/lect11d-chromo_alter_1-deletions/duplication_overview.drawn.svg)

A duplication repeats a segment.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Inversion*

@body

![Slide image 3](assets/lect11d-chromo_alter_1-deletions/inversion_overview.drawn.svg)

An inversion reverses a segment within a chromosome.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Translocation*

@body

![Slide image 4](assets/lect11d-chromo_alter_1-deletions/translocation_overview.drawn.svg)

A translocation moves a segment from one chromosome to a nonhomologous chromosome.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Human Disorders Due to Chromosomal Alterations*

@body

- Alterations of chromosome number and structure are associated with some serious disorders
- Some types of aneuploidy appear to upset the genetic balance less than others, resulting in individuals surviving to birth and beyond
- These surviving individuals have a set of symptoms, or syndrome, characteristic of the type of aneuploidy

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

One possible result of chromosomal breakage is for a fragment to join a non-homologous chromosome. What is this alteration called?

- A) deletion
- B) inversion
- C) translocation
- D) duplication

@answer

[Answer\: C]{color=red}

=== layout: section

# Deletions

=== layout: two-panels

# *Deletion Detection in Humans*

@left

![SNP arrays and comparative genomic hybridization workflows](assets/lect11d-chromo_alter_1-deletions/array_comparison_workflow.drawn.svg)

![Original measured copy-number trace](assets/lect11d-chromo_alter_1-deletions/array_trace.evidence.png)

@right

- Deletions can be detected in humans with 23andMe like SNP microarray chips
- If regions of SNPs are missing from the analysis, a deletion is likely present.

=== layout: two-panels

# *Disorders Caused by Deletions*

@left

- The syndrome cri du chat (&#8220;cry of the cat&#8221;), results from a specific deletion in chromosome 5
  - A child born with this syndrome is severely intellectually disabled and has a catlike cry; individuals usually die in infancy or early childhood

@right

![Slide image 6](assets/lect11d-chromo_alter_1-deletions/image_006.png)

![Slide image 7](assets/lect11d-chromo_alter_1-deletions/image_007.png)

=== layout: section

# Deletions\: how do they happen?

=== layout: two-panels

# *Deletions*

@left

- Deletions are generally harmful.
  - the larger the deletion the greater the harm.
- Formed in two ways\:
  - chromosome breakage in two places and fused together with piece left out.
  - ectopic recombination &#8211; recombination with repeated sequences on same chromosome.

@right

![Deletion gene order](assets/lect11d-chromo_alter_1-deletions/deletion_overview.drawn.svg)

=== layout: one-panel

# *Ectopic Recombination*

@body

![Direct repeats recombine and release an acentric ring](assets/lect11d-chromo_alter_1-deletions/direct_repeat_loop.drawn.svg)

=== layout: section

# Deletion Detection in Drosophila

=== layout: two-panels

# *Deletion Mutant with wildtype Phenotype*

@left

![Slide image 9](assets/lect11d-chromo_alter_1-deletions/deletion_parent.drawn.svg)

@right

- In lab settings, deletions can be detected by missing _wildtype_ alleles in heterozygous organisms or by a test-cross.
- Once a deletion is found, its size can be estimated by determining which genes were in the deleted region.

